4. Write a short scenario describing how a message sent from your device to a friend's device on WhatsApp travels through each OSI layer, mentioning what happens at each step.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Start from Application layer (your message in WhatsApp) down to Physical layer (actual data sent over WiFi/mobile network), then back up to Application layer on your friend's phone.</em>
=>You type “Hi” in WhatsApp and hit Send. Your friend’s phone receives it.
Down the stack on your phone (Encapsulation):

Application (Layer 7) – WhatsApp app takes your text “Hi”, adds its own headers (message ID, chat ID, encryption keys) and prepares the data to be sent.
Presentation (Layer 6) – The message is encrypted (end-to-end encryption using the Signal protocol / TLS-like mechanisms) and possibly compressed.
Session (Layer 5) – A session is maintained with WhatsApp’s servers (or the relay servers) so the connection stays alive.
Transport (Layer 4) – The encrypted data is placed inside a TCP segment (reliable delivery, ports are chosen).
Network (Layer 3) – A source IP (your phone’s IP) and destination IP (WhatsApp server or relay) are added → IP packet.
Data Link (Layer 2) – The IP packet is framed for your current network (Wi-Fi frame or mobile network frame) with MAC addresses.
Physical (Layer 1) – The bits are turned into radio waves and transmitted over Wi-Fi or the mobile (4G/5G) network.

Across the network:

The packets travel through your router → ISP → internet routers → WhatsApp’s servers (or directly peer-to-peer in some cases) → your friend’s ISP → friend’s phone.
Up the stack on your friend’s phone (Decapsulation):

Physical – Radio waves are received and turned back into bits.
Data Link – Frame is checked and the IP packet is extracted.
Network – IP header is examined; packet is delivered to the correct device.
Transport – TCP reassembles the segments in order.
Session – Session information is restored.
Presentation – Data is decrypted.
Application – WhatsApp app receives the plaintext “Hi” and shows the message on the screen.
